From d997a53c6014b45eaf0ac2b966e18577d50e9d36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 18:43:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] net: compress blocks once for P2P WebSocket peers that
 negotiate it

A p2p-zstd-v1 WebSocket connection compresses one zstd stream per
direction. Its output depends on the connection's history, so a block
sent to N WebSocket peers is compressed N times. Compression is most of
what a node spends per WebSocket peer when it serves a block (2.2-2.7
ns/byte against 0.2 for serialization and 0.4 for the checksum), so a new
tip served to many peers costs N times that.

Port the V2 tier's independent block frames (V2 patch 095, "[v2 only]
net: cache dictionary-versioned independent compressed block frames") to
the eCash tier, negotiated so that upgraded and older ecashd nodes keep
interoperating:

- Negotiation: a new Sec-WebSocket-Protocol token, p2p-zstd-v1-frames
  (p2p-zstd-v1 plus independent frames). Dialers offer it first by
  default. Servers that support it select it whenever it is offered;
  older servers ignore the unknown token and select p2p-zstd-v1, and
  older dialers never offer it. Both ends know the outcome before the
  first P2P message. Only with p2p-zstd-v1-frames may either end send an
  RSV2 frame, and only then does a decoder accept one; otherwise any RSV
  bit still ends the connection. The Beast bridge (-p2pwebsocketbridge)
  never selects it. Raw TCP, p2p-zstd, none and permessage-deflate are
  unchanged.
- Format (as V2): an unfragmented binary message with RSV2 only, payload
  [format 1][dictionary version LE32][one zstd frame with its content
  size], compressed with only the dictionary. Version 1 is the existing
  p2p-zstd-v1 dictionary. It decompresses to whole P2P messages and
  neither uses nor changes the stream state.
- Decoder: V2's strict validation: unknown format or dictionary
  version, skippable, concatenated, trailing or truncated data, missing
  or over-limit content size, and a declared window above 1 MiB (checked
  before allocating) are fatal, and nothing is appended on error.
- Sender: WebSocketSock follows the outgoing P2P message framing (CConnman
  sends a header with MSG_MORE, then the payload) and sends `block`
  messages of at least -p2pcompressoncethreshold bytes (default 65536,
  header included; 0 never sends frames but still accepts them) as one
  independent frame, after framing whatever precedes them. A header split
  by a flush, or any other unusual send pattern, falls back to the stream;
  the P2P bytes delivered are the same either way.
- Cache: one LRU shared by all peers (net_zstd_independent.*), bounded by
  the allocated bytes of its frames (-p2pcompressoncecache, MiB, default
  128) and 64 blocks. The key is the block hash, the exact 24-byte P2P
  header (magic, command, length and the payload's checksum, which the
  serializer has already computed) and the dictionary version, so two
  serializations under one block hash never share bytes; V2 hashed the
  whole payload again for this instead. Peers asking for a block while
  it is being compressed wait for that compression (a shared future, not
  V2's cache-wide lock), so unrelated blocks and hits are not blocked.
- Metrics (lotus_, this tier's prefix): independent-frame peers (gauge),
  frames sent/received, cache hits, misses, evictions, bytes, entries
  and compress-once encodes. getpeerinfo shows websocket
  independent_frames and frames sent/received.
- doc/p2p-websocket.md documents the eCash rule in place of V2's "not
  suitable for the eCash tier" note.

Evidence:

- Unit tests (net_websocket_tests, 30 cases): negotiation, including an
  older server given an upgraded offer and a client offering only the
  new token; stream history kept across frames and a fresh peer decoding
  a frame alone; RSV2 refused unless negotiated and every invalid RSV
  combination; strict validation cases with nothing appended; the
  message limit; cache bounds, identity, eviction and a single
  compression for 8 concurrent requests; mixed peers (3 with frames, 2
  without) receiving identical streams from one compression; split
  headers, multi-call payloads, large non-block messages, thresholds and
  frames sent by clients.
- p2p_websocket_compress_once.py: node-to-node negotiation (default
  dialer gets frames, a dialer offering only p2p-zstd-v1 does not); one
  node serves a block to two frames peers, a p2p-zstd-v1 peer, an
  uncompressed peer and a raw TCP peer, which all receive identical
  bytes; one encode, one miss, one hit, two frames sent (metrics); a
  second request is a hit; a negotiated peer may send a frame, an
  un-negotiated one is disconnected.
- Existing p2p_websocket_{inbound,discovery,tls,framework,proxy} and
  interface_http_{metrics,publicbind} pass.

A/B (contrib/bench/ws-block-frames, previous commit): A = base, B = this
commit, all peers offering p2p-zstd-v1-frames,p2p-zstd-v1,p2p-zstd (A
